  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.40.8.55", "inferred_role": "public_facing_iis_web_frontend_compromised", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:43:08+0100", "last_seen": "2027-02-26T01:49:08+0100", "agent_name": "WEB-FRONT-02.corp.local"},
    {"ip": "45.95.169.142", "inferred_role": "external_c2_callback_destination", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:47:52+0100", "last_seen": "2027-02-26T01:47:52+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / Web Shell Execution", "technique": "T1505.003 + T1059.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-02-26 01:43:08 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WEB-FRONT-02.corp.local (10.40.8.55): image=powershell.exe, commandLine='powershell.exe -nop -enc <base64>', parentImage=w3wp.exe (IIS worker process). PowerShell launched as a CHILD of the IIS w3wp.exe worker = textbook web-shell-triggered command execution. The IIS worker should NEVER spawn powershell.exe under normal operation. The base64-encoded payload (-enc) is the staging command for the rest of the attack chain.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=w3wp.exe + image=powershell.exe + '-enc' flag = web shell command execution. user context is CORP\\svc-backup01 (the IIS app pool identity).", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:43:08+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution / Staging (Binary Drop)", "technique": "T1105", "what_happened": "At 01:45:22, Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) on WEB-FRONT-02.corp.local: targetFilename=C:\\ProgramData\\wuhelper.exe, signed=false, hash=d0c8a3a91a8e3a45afef02e84e5f6b3c7c8d9e0f1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f, user=CORP\\svc-backup01. An UNSIGNED binary was dropped into C:\\ProgramData (a writable-by-all directory commonly abused for staging) by the same account that runs the IIS worker. The filename 'wuhelper.exe' attempts to masquerade as a Windows Update helper — a known LOLBin masquerading pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename ends with .exe, signed=false, location=C:\\ProgramData\\ (non-standard install path), user=svc-backup01 (an IIS service account that should NEVER be writing executables to ProgramData).", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:45:22+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence (Service Installation — T1543.003)", "technique": "T1543.003", "what_happened": "At 01:47:33, Windows Security Event 7045 'A new service was installed' fired on WEB-FRONT-02.corp.local: ServiceName=WinUpdateHelper, ImagePath=C:\\ProgramData\\wuhelper.exe, ServiceType='user mode service', StartType='auto start', AccountName=LocalSystem, SubjectUserName=svc-backup01. One second later (01:47:34), Event 4697 'A service was installed in the system' fired with the same content (the paired audit event). At 01:47:36, Event 7036 fired 'service WinUpdateHelper entered the running state'. THIS IS THE CORE T1543.003 SIGNATURE: (a) unsigned binary at C:\\ProgramData\\, (b) service installed pointing to it, (c) StartType=auto (survives reboots), (d) AccountName=LocalSystem (highest local privilege), (e) installed by a NON-admin service account (svc-backup01) — privilege escalation from service-account context to SYSTEM via the service-install primitive.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 7045 + 4697 + 7036 trio on the same host within 3 seconds, ServiceName + ImagePath pointing to the freshly-dropped unsigned binary, AccountName=LocalSystem auto-start. The unsigned-image + ProgramData-path + LocalSystem-context combination is the diagnostic signature of malicious service-based persistence.", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:47:33+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Execution as SYSTEM (Service Start)", "technique": "T1543.003 + T1078", "what_happened": "At 01:47:36, Sysmon Event 1 fired: image=C:\\ProgramData\\wuhelper.exe, parentImage=services.exe, user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. The freshly-installed service launched its image with full SYSTEM privileges via the standard services.exe → service-image parent-child relationship. The attacker has now elevated from svc-backup01 (limited service-account context) to NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM on the IIS frontend — the persistence touch is also a privilege escalation.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=services.exe + image points to a ProgramData-located unsigned executable + user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. services.exe legitimately spawns service images, but those images should be signed Microsoft binaries under C:\\Windows\\System32 or signed third-party software under C:\\Program Files — never an unsigned binary in ProgramData.", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:47:36+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command & Control (C2 Beacon)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1573", "what_happened": "At 01:47:52 (16 seconds after service start), Sysmon Event 3 (network connect) fired: image=C:\\ProgramData\\wuhelper.exe → destinationIp=45.95.169.142 destinationPort=443 (TLS). The persisted service immediately reached out to an external IP on 443 — the C2 callback. 16 seconds from service-start to first C2 beacon is the classic 'install-and-callback' implant pattern.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=wuhelper.exe + destinationIp NOT in RFC1918 + destinationPort=443 (TLS-encrypted to evade content inspection). The freshly-installed service contacting an external IP within seconds of start = C2 channel established.", "first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:47:52+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Discovery / Privilege Enumeration", "technique": "T1033 + T1087.002", "what_happened": "At 01:48:14, Sysmon Event 1: image=cmd.exe + commandLine='cmd.exe /c whoami /priv', parentImage=C:\\ProgramData\\wuhelper.exe, user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. The implant launched the canonical Windows privilege enumeration command — SeDebugPrivilege / SeImpersonatePrivilege / SeBackupPrivilege are the attacker-interesting ones; under SYSTEM, all are present and the implant is verifying its own elevated state. At 01:49:08, a second discovery command: image=net.exe + commandLine='net group \"Domain Admins\" /domain', parentImage=wuhelper.exe = domain-account enumeration to identify the next target list. The cmd.exe + net.exe children of an unsigned ProgramData binary running as SYSTEM = hands-off-keyboard automated implant orientation.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=wuhelper.exe (the persisted service binary) + image in (cmd.exe, net.exe) + commandLine matches the post-elevation discovery signature (whoami /priv → net group Domain Admins /domain). The service-spawning-discovery-tools chain is diagnostic of implant orientation, not legitimate service behavior.","first_seen": "2027-02-26T01:48:14+0100"}
  ],
